<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Christopher Columbus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1451">1451</a>–<a href="/years/1506">1506</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Columbus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Christopher Columbus was a Genoese navigator whose voyage of 1492, funded by the Castilian crown, reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>. His voyages initiated Spanish colonisation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>. He sought a western route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, inspired in part by accounts of the court of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a> such as that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus.jpg" alt="Christopher Columbus" width="320" height="387" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sebastiano del Piombo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_a_Man,_Said_to_be_Christopher_Columbus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Scholars agree that Columbus was born in the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, between 25 August and 31 October 1451; theories placing his origin in Aragon or Portugal are discounted by most of them. His father, Domenico Colombo, was a wool weaver who worked in Genoa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savona">Savona</a> and owned a cheese stand where the boy worked. His mother was Susanna Fontanarossa. In 1470 the family moved to Savona, where Domenico took over a tavern. Columbus wrote that he went to sea at the age of 14, and he later settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, where his brother Bartholomew ran a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cartography">cartography</a> workshop.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in 1453, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> was closed to Christian traders. In 1474 the Florentine astronomer Paolo dal Pozzo Toscanelli suggested to King Afonso V of Portugal that sailing west across the Atlantic would reach Asia faster than the route around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, but the king rejected the idea. Columbus and his brother proposed such a plan in the 1480s, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolomeu-dias">Bartolomeu Dias</a>'s rounding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a> in 1488 complicated it. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada-war">Granada War</a> and years of lobbying, Queen Isabella I and King Ferdinand II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> agreed to sponsor the voyage.</p><p>Columbus left the Port of Palos in August 1492 with three ships and made landfall on 12 October on an island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahamas">Bahamas</a> known to its inhabitants as Guanahani. He then visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>, where he founded the short-lived fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-navidad">La Navidad</a>, and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> in early 1493 with captured American Indians. Three further voyages took him to the Lesser Antilles in 1493, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a> and the northern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> in 1498, and to the east coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a> in 1502. He never clearly renounced his belief that he had reached the Far East, and he called the people he met indios.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>As colonial governor Columbus was accused by some contemporaries of significant brutality, and he was arrested and removed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a> in 1500. He afterwards demanded the tenth of the new lands' wealth promised in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitulations-of-santa-fe">Capitulations of Santa Fe</a>, and after his death his heirs pursued the claim in the protracted lawsuits known as the pleitos colombinos. His remains were moved several times, between Valladolid, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> and perhaps <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a>, and DNA tests on those in Seville in 2003 led researchers to conclude that they are his.</p><p>His voyages began lasting contact between the two hemispheres and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbian-exchange">Columbian exchange</a> of plants, animals, diseases and ideas. Diseases and warfare introduced from the Old World contributed to the decline of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>'s American Indians, who were also enslaved and mistreated under Columbus's government. Once idealised as a heroic discoverer, he is now examined more critically.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Christopher Columbus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its crown sponsored <b>Christopher Columbus</b> under the Capitulations of Santa Fe in 1492, divided the non-European world with Portugal by the Treaty of Tordesillas in 1494, and treated the Americas as Castilian possessions: the Council of the Indies, founded in 1524, governed them under Castilian law, and Castilian…</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Christopher Columbus</b>, born in Genoa around 1451, came out of this world of Genoese Atlantic trade, and Genoese merchants in Seville financed early Spanish voyages and the sugar economy of the Atlantic islands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Republic of Genoa · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Columbus</b>'s first voyage, sponsored by the Castilian crown and departing in August 1492, began the most consequential sequence of territorial acquisitions in imperial history.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Marco Polo's account of China, whether accurate in every detail or embellished, introduced European readers to the wealth, population and technological achievements of Chinese civilisation and fed the desire for direct access to Asian commerce; <b>Columbus</b> owned and annotated a copy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Christopher Columbus</b>’s expedition founded a Spanish colony on Hispaniola in 1492 and the Taíno population was decimated, colonists began importing enslaved West Africans around 1512; some were priests of African traditional religions, others had already absorbed Catholic influences or were Muslim.</blockquote><a href="/religion/haitian-vodou">Haitian Vodou · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The English word “cannibal” comes from the Kalinago word karibna, “person”, after <b>Columbus</b> spread stories of man-eating Caribs that he had apparently heard from their Taíno enemies.</blockquote><a href="/culture/kalinago">Kalinago · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Genoa, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valladolid">Valladolid</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.41,8.93&amp;year=1486" aria-label="Genoa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4764 593 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Christopher Columbus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5214 863h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5214 863h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.41,8.93&amp;year=1486">What was here in 1486? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Gold is the most precious of all commodities; gold constitutes treasure, and he who possesses it has all he needs in this world, as also the means of rescuing souls from purgatory, and restoring them to the enjoyment of paradise.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter on the fourth voyage (Jamaica, 1503) &#183; tr. R. H. Major, 1847 (2nd ed. 1870) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/77820/pg77820-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Weep for me, whoever has charity, truth, and justice! I did not come out on this voyage to gain to myself honour or wealth…&#8221;</p><footer>Letter on the fourth voyage (Jamaica, 1503) &#183; tr. R. H. Major, 1847 (2nd ed. 1870) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/77820/pg77820-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;But the more I reason on the subject, the more satisfied I become that the terrestrial paradise is situated in the spot I have described; and I ground my opinion upon the arguments and authorities already quoted.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter on the third voyage (1498) &#183; tr. R. H. Major, 1847 (2nd ed. 1870) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/77820/pg77820-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…they are guileless, and so liberal of all they have that no one would believe it who had not seen it. They never refuse anything that they possess when it is asked of them…&#8221;</p><footer>Letter on the first voyage (1493) &#183; tr. R. H. Major, 1847 (2nd ed. 1870) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/77820/pg77820-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I believe it is impossible to ascend thither, because I am convinced that it is the spot of the earthly paradise…&#8221;</p><footer>Letter on the third voyage (1498) &#183; tr. R. H. Major, 1847 (2nd ed. 1870) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/77820/pg77820-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;For seven years was I at your royal court, where every one to whom the enterprise was mentioned, treated it as ridiculous; but now there is not a man, down to the very tailors, who does not beg to be allowed to become a discoverer.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter on the fourth voyage (Jamaica, 1503) &#183; tr. R. H. Major, 1847 (2nd ed. 1870) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/77820/pg77820-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
